<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trucial States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trucial States were the sheikhdoms of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast that signed maritime truces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1820 onwards, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpetual-maritime-truce">Perpetual Maritime Truce</a> of 1853 and the Exclusive Agreement of 1892, which made them British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in all but name. Britain handled their foreign relations and defence until it withdrew in 1971, when six of them formed the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> joined in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trucial-states.jpg" alt="Trucial States" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Svyatoslav · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Trucial_States_(1968%E2%80%931971).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
